    "q-345593": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345593",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each athlete among a group of athletes. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular athlete.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.5,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.1.\n$$\n\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2}+(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =30.26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the scientist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{29}{30.26} \\\\  & ≈0.958 \\\\  & =95.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
