    "q-345609": {
      "dom_id": "step-q345609",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "An economist measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each currency in a collection under devaluation. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular currency.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=12,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe economist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =203\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the economist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2} \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{144}{203} \\\\  & ≈0.709 \\\\  & =70.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
